  "classIri": "urn:ngm:class:pervasive-computing",
  "domain": "infrastructure",
  "definition": "Pervasive computing \u2014 also termed ubiquitous computing \u2014 is a paradigm in which computational capability is embedded throughout the physical environment and integrated seamlessly into everyday objects and infrastructure, such that computation becomes a background utility rather than a discrete user activity. Coined by Mark Weiser at Xerox PARC in 1991, the vision encompasses context-aware, networked devices that sense, process, and communicate without explicit user interaction. It is the conceptual precursor to the Internet of Things and ambient intelligence.",
